customer_jordan: Hey team — we've started seeing intermittent `Unexpected end of JSON input` errors in our Next.js app when consuming SSE from the Redwood streaming endpoint. Happens after ~10-30s of streaming, sometimes the last chunk reappears and we get duplicated tokens. We're behind Cloudflare (Workers + CDN) and using an EventSource polyfill (eventsource-parser + fetch). Repro steps below, happy to DM logs.
maria-support: Sorry you're hitting this — can you paste a minimal repro and indicate whether you can reproduce with `curl --http2` vs `--http1.1`? Also send the exact request headers (no secrets) and one example request-id header if available.
customer_jordan: Sure — here's a tiny repro we used locally against the public endpoint (token redacted):
```bash
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-REDACTED" -H "Accept: text/event-stream" --http2 -N "https://api.redwood.example/v1/stream?model=redwood-alpha"
```
Observed output (trimmed):
```
data: {"id":"tok_1","text":"Hello"}

data: {"id":"tok_2","text":"Worl

data: {"id":"tok_2","text":"World"}
```
The middle line breaks in the middle of the JSON payload (`Worl`) and then the client receives a follow-up event with the same id and the rest of the payload. That causes our JSON parser to crash in some codepaths and also duplicates the token in our UI. When we force `--http1.1` we rarely see the split; with `--http2` it reproduces ~30% of runs. We also reproduced by toggling Cloudflare off and then it's much less frequent.
sam-sdk: Thanks — this looks like SSE framing splitting over HTTP/2 DATA frames and the edge replaying or reordering chunks during a midstream reconnect. Two quick notes:
- SSE requires events to be separated by a newline (or `\n\n` for event termination). If a proxy or HTTP/2 segmentation splits a JSON object across frames, naive parsers that assume each `data:` line is a full JSON blob will break. The safe client approach is to accumulate `data:` lines until an empty-line separator and only then join and JSON.parse. Many polyfills do this but some minimal consumers parse per-line.
- Another vector: if a token rotation / auth refresh triggers a retry at the edge, it can re-send a partially-sent event which looks like a duplicate id and partial JSON.
Workarounds to try now:

1) Client-side: buffer `data:` lines until you get the blank-line delimiter, then `JSON.parse(buffer.join("\n"))`. Example in Node:

```js
// naive eventsource parser pattern
let buf = [];
parser.on('data', line => {
  if (line === '') {
    const eventText = buf.join('\n');
    try { const obj = JSON.parse(eventText); /* handle obj */ } catch(e) { /* log, buffer until complete */ }
    buf = [];
  } else {
    buf.push(line.replace(/^data:\s*/, ''));
  }
});
```

2) Reproduce locally with `curl --http2 -N` (you already did) and with `--http1.1` to confirm HTTP/2 correlation.

3) If you can, capture the `X-Request-ID` / `X-Edge-Id` header from responses and paste it here — that'll let us look up the edge logs faster.
maria-support: tagging @alex-engruntime for infra/runtime input — we need to know whether the edge is coalescing or retransmitting DATA frames on auth retry.
alex-engruntime: Looking at prior incidents, our HTTP/2 edge sometimes splits large payloads into frames that get retransmitted if the connection resets midstream (especially with edge-level token rotation). Two immediate mitigations:

- Quick server-side: ensure each SSE event is terminated with a double newline and consider adding a small per-event heartbeat (`:hb\n`) to keep the connection alive and reduce replays.
- Medium: we can add a short-term proxy config tweak to reduce Nagle-style coalescing and to avoid re-sending partially-flushed frames on downstream auth refresh.

I'll open INF-3245 to track the edge config change and a follow-up hotfix. Can you share one `X-Request-ID` for a failed run and the approximate time? I'll correlate in the edge logs.

ci-bot: INF-3245 created: https://redwood.atlassian.net/browse/INF-3245 (edge http2 frame handling + streaming de-dup), linked to log bundle. Assigned to eng-runtime.

customer_jordan: Provided `X-Request-ID: rq_2026-03-17-7a1b2c` and timestamp `2026-03-17T15:42:18Z`. One more note — we saw billing duplicates for a few retries (token counted twice). Our finance team is asking if duplicates like this can be excluded from usage until root cause fixed.

maria-support: Understood, we'll open a billing review once eng confirms cause. For now, please try the buffering client workaround (should be low-effort) and confirm if it eliminates the parser exception. We'll track INF-3245 and follow up with a patch ETA later today.

sam-sdk: also adding a client SDK short-term patch that hardens the streaming parser (v1.2.3-beta): it buffers lines and dedups events by `id` for the session. If you want, I can share the npm tarball link for you to test quickly.

customer_jordan: Would appreciate the beta tarball. Buffering client-side removed the parser crash in our QA runs (no duplicates so far). Thank you both.

maria-support: Great — marking as ongoing. Action items: eng-runtime (alex) investigate INF-3245 and edge logs, sam to ship SDK beta and share link, support to follow up with billing once eng confirms duplication source. We'll update here when we have the edge log correlation.

customer_jordan: thanks, will test beta and report back. :thumbsup:
